{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  // The dedicated jobs Worker. Its `name` is rewritten by scripts/setup-cloudflare.ts to match
  // CLOUDFLARE_WORKER_NAME (with a `-jobs` suffix), so one deployment keeps consistent names.
  //
  // `main` is the standalone Vite bundle (see vite.jobs-worker.config.mts), not a `.ts` source: the
  // job handler graph must ship without the Next.js server runtime, so it is bundled by the same
  // `vite-tsconfig-paths` pipeline the authzed CLI and env-validator use, then handed to wrangler as a
  // single ESM file.
  "name": "formbricks-worker-jobs",
  "main": "dist/jobs-worker/index.mjs",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-28",
  // `no_handle_cross_request_promise_resolution` is load-bearing here, not cosmetic. The app's Prisma
  // client is a module-scope singleton wrapping a `pg.Pool`, so its TCP connections outlive the queue
  // invocation that opened them and are reused by a later one. The socket's `data` listener — and thus
  // pg's `handleReadyForQuery`, which resolves the query promise — was registered in the *first*
  // invocation's request context, so workerd (since compat date 2024-10-14) cancels that continuation:
  // the awaited query never settles, the job eventually exceeds the consumer's wall-time budget, the
  // whole batch is redelivered and the dead-letter queue grows. Disabling the cross-request
  // cancellation is the fix the runtime itself recommends in the warning this produced.
  "compatibility_flags": [
    "nodejs_compat",
    "global_fetch_strictly_public",
    "no_handle_cross_request_promise_resolution"
  ],
  "minify": true,
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  // A direct TCP connection from the Worker to Neon's inter-region endpoint has to complete a cold
  // handshake on every new client, which intermittently exceeds the pool's connect timeout and fails
  // the job with `timeout exceeded when trying to connect`. Hyperdrive keeps a warm, pooled connection
  // on Cloudflare's side so the Worker dials a local endpoint instead. `worker-env.ts` copies the
  // binding's `connectionString` onto `DATABASE_URL`, taking precedence over the secret so every job
  // handler lands on Hyperdrive without changing how the app reads its config.
  "hyperdrive": [
    {
      "binding": "HYPERDRIVE",
      "id": "fee3147d8c9b4fac81caa48866ce6d6e"
    }
  ],
  // The queue this Worker both consumes from and produces to: consuming is the job dispatch itself,
  // and the producer binding is what lets a handler fan out child jobs (a response-pipeline job
  // enqueues one webhook-delivery job per matching webhook; the reconciler re-dispatches workflow
  // runs). `max_batch_size` is deliberately small: the consumer handles a batch's messages serially,
  // and a response-pipeline job is expensive (several DB round-trips plus email rendering), so a
  // large batch blows the Worker's wall-time budget and the whole batch is redelivered forever,
  // stalling the queue. Three jobs per invocation keeps a batch inside that budget. `max_retries` is
  // the outer bound on redelivery before a message goes to the dead-letter queue.
  "queues": {
    "producers": [
      {
        "binding": "JOBS_QUEUE",
        "queue": "formbricks-jobs"
      }
    ],
    "consumers": [
      {
        "queue": "formbricks-jobs",
        "max_batch_size": 3,
        "max_batch_timeout": 5,
        "max_retries": 5,
        "dead_letter_queue": "formbricks-jobs-dlq"
      }
    ]
  },
  // No `triggers.crons` here, and that is the point: a Workers Free account may hold five Cron Triggers
  // in total, per *account*, and this account's five are held by other Workers — declaring any cron made
  // every deploy fail with `code: 10072`. The recurring jobs are driven by the Durable Object below
  // instead, whose alarms have no such budget. See lib/jobs/cf-schedules.ts for the cadences.
  //
  // `exports` is the declarative way to declare a Durable Object class (it replaces the legacy
  // `migrations` array, and the two are mutually exclusive). New namespaces created this way always use
  // the SQLite storage backend, which is the only one available on the Workers Free plan.
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      {
        "name": "JOBS_SCHEDULER",
        "class_name": "JobsScheduler"
      }
    ]
  },
  "exports": {
    "JobsScheduler": {
      "type": "durable-object",
      "storage": "sqlite"
    }
  },
  "vars": {
    "NODE_ENV": "production",
    "FORMBRICKS_JOBS_ENGINE": "cloudflare"
  }
}